{"parentUuid":"62b39ca6-c5e7-481a-bd56-291707df06e0","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChLP8j2jv6FZx2HsCEfb4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01K762XEVvdb6WCVGaNoQPpY","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Plan VR-2 verify for clients","subagent_type":"architect","prompt":"Plan unit VR-2 of the Nexus Code v1.3.0 release. Write the plan to `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/VR2_PLAN.md` (you may create only that file; do not edit code). Work read-only in the worktree `/home/mike/nexus-wt/vr2` (branch `v1/vr2-verify-for-clients`, cut from `origin/release/v1.3.0`). Do not ask the user questions: where something is undecided, pick the conservative default, record it under \"Decisions taken\", and move on.\n\nThe unit, verbatim from `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` (read sections around it too): \"VR-2 | Expose verification as an MCP tool and an ACP extension so another harness can call Nexus to check its own work (`serve-mcp` today exposes workspace tools, not verification); the caller can read the verdict but cannot change it | acceptance: an external client's session gets a verdict and a receipt id; tampering with the request cannot raise the verdict | M | P1 (decision D20, default: yes)\".\n\nWhat exists (verify each by reading; cite file:line in the plan):\n- VR-1 delivered `nexus verify` (a CLI command and core in `cli/src/core/verify.ts` and neighbours; plan at `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/VR1_PLAN.md`; evidence docs `docs/evidence/REVIEW_FIXES_01.md`, `REVIEW_FIXES_02.md`, `VR1C_FIXES.md`). It produces a verify result with an outcome ladder (VERIFIED / NOT_ESTABLISHED / …), schema `docs/protocol/verify-result-v3.schema.json`, and receipts. PRs #732 and #737 hardened it against forged evidence (fixture independence, hashed inputs, snapshot handling). VR-3 (#745) added sandbox denial events to receipts.\n- `nexus serve-mcp` (find it: `cli/src/index.ts` and the MCP server module) exposes workspace tools over MCP.\n- The ACP adapter `nexus acp` (`cli/src/acp_stdio.ts`, `cli/src/acp/{types,jsonrpc,validate,mapping}.ts`, `docs/ACP.md`, plan `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/AC1_PLAN.md`) already has one extension method, `_nexus/receipt/get`, and sends a verification update at the end of each prompt.\n- Managed policy enforcement: `cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts`. Workspace trust: `cli/src/workspace_trust.ts`.\n\nThe plan must settle, with reasons:\n1. The MCP tool: name, input schema, output shape (verdict, reason, receipt id, schema version), and exactly which inputs the caller controls. The central security property: the caller is UNTRUSTED. It must not be able to choose the verifier command, weaken the checks, point verification at another directory, supply its own \"evidence\", raise an outcome, or get a verdict for code other than what is on disk in the served workspace. Derive from how `nexus verify` decides its verifier (project-declared command, discovered verifier with approval digest, `--verifier` operator override) which of those an MCP caller may trigger; the conservative answer is: only what the operator who started `serve-mcp` has already configured/approved; anything needing a decision returns NOT_ESTABLISHED (or the existing \"needs approval\" outcome) and never prompts the caller. State what happens in an untrusted workspace and in read-only mode, and whether running a verifier (which executes project code) is acceptable under `serve-mcp`'s existing posture (read how serve-mcp gates `run_command`-like tools today).\n2. The ACP extension: method name (keep the existing `_nexus/` prefix constant), params, result, and how it relates to `_nexus/receipt/get` and to the per-prompt verification update. Same untrusted-caller rules. Concurrency with a running prompt on the same session (refuse while a turn runs?).\n3. Receipt identity: where the receipt is stored, how the id is formed, how a caller reads it back, and why a caller cannot forge or overwrite one.\n4. Resource bounds: timeouts, one verification at a time per workspace/session, output size caps, cancellation.\n5. Reuse: call the existing verify core; no second verification path. List the functions to call and what (if anything) must be refactored out of the CLI command to be callable.\n6. Tests that prove the acceptance and the tamper cases: a scripted MCP client and a scripted ACP client each get a verdict and receipt id; hostile requests (extra fields, a verifier command, a path outside the workspace, a claimed outcome, replayed/forged receipt ids, oversized input, concurrent calls) cannot raise the verdict; a seeded failing verifier is not VERIFIED; a weakened-test diff is not VERIFIED (reuse VR-1 fixtures). Name the test files and the helpers to reuse (`cli/test/helpers/`).\n7. Docs to update (`docs/ACP.md`, the MCP server doc, `docs/PROTOCOL.md`, user manual, schema/version ledgers such as `docs/protocol/VERSIONS.json`), and repo gates that will notice (requirements ledger `scripts/check_requirements.mjs`, `scripts/check_invariants.mjs`, tool-description/prompt budget tests such as `cli/test/context_convergence.test.ts` if a model-facing tool list changes — note serve-mcp tools may or may not count; check).\n8. Slices: propose 2–3 mergeable slices (e.g. shared callable verify entry + MCP tool; ACP extension; docs/evidence), each with files touched, tests, and a rough size. Flag anything that would collide with files other agents hold: check live claims in `/home/mike/nexus-code/.git/nexus-claims/` (directories without `.released-` in the name; read their `owner.json` scope).\n\nAlso record: reference-coding note per `docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md` (what peer projects do for \"verify as a tool\", or \"XERJ unavailable: <reason>\" plus `git grep` findings — do not spend long on this), risks, and what is explicitly out of scope.\n\nShell notes: zsh (use `bash -c` for loops); output is filtered by `rtk` (prefix `rtk proxy` when a grep/sed pipeline looks odd). Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus. No live inference.\n\nReturn (under 500 words): the path of the plan, the decisions taken, the slice list with files, the main risks, and anything you could not determine."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1283,"cache_read_input_tokens":370885,"output_tokens":2364,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1283,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2364,"cache_read_input_tokens":370885,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1283,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1283},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":24800,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01K762XEVvdb6WCVGaNoQPpY":{"description":"Plan VR-2 verify for clients","subagent_type":"architect","prompt":"Plan unit VR-2 of the Nexus Code v1.3.0 release. Write the plan to `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/VR2_PLAN.md` (you may create only that file; do not edit code). Work read-only in the worktree `/home/mike/nexus-wt/vr2` (branch `v1/vr2-verify-for-clients`, cut from `origin/release/v1.3.0`). Do not ask the user questions: where something is undecided, pick the conservative default, record it under \"Decisions taken\", and move on.\n\nThe unit, verbatim from `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` (read sections around it too): \"VR-2 | Expose verification as an MCP tool and an ACP extension so another harness can call Nexus to check its own work (`serve-mcp` today exposes workspace tools, not verification); the caller can read the verdict but cannot change it | acceptance: an external client's session gets