{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-23T20:27:43.589Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a560b8addcc5bbf8d</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01XHntFFBgxw6L7YiTEtcPcg</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a560b8addcc5bbf8d.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review VR-2 slice 1 PR\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS** at `4196a306c97152ff735498d7c6bdb5a0bcdd0c75` (PR #756, base `release/v1.3.0`). The head did not move. I found no way to get VERIFIED for a weakened or broken change. One real defect: SIGTERM of the server leaks the verifier and scratch dirs.\n\n**Findings, ranked**\n\n1. **Non-blocking, confirmed by running.** `cli/src/index.ts:~1195-1217` and `cli/src/core/verify_service.ts:forkRunner`: a SIGTERM to the real server leaves the worker's verifier running and the scratch trees on disk. serve-mcp has no SIGTERM handler, and `process.once(\"exit\")` does not fire on signal death. I ran the server directly under node with a SIGTERM-ignoring verifier mid-run. After SIGTERM, `sleep` and `bwrap` survived and `nexus-verify-*` and `nexus-verifier-*` dirs remained. SIGKILL of the server behaves the same.\n   - Fix: add SIGTERM and SIGINT handlers that call `server.shutdown()` and wait for the runner to settle.\n   - Stdin EOF and `notifications/cancelled` are clean: processes gone in about 1s, no scratch left, and no response after cancel.\n2. **Non-blocking, by reading.** `mcp.ts` `startStdio` (the added `void this.handleMessage(req).then(...)`) has no `.catch`. A throw in that path would be an unhandled rejection and could crash the server. Add a catch that writes a JSON-RPC error.\n3. **Non-blocking, by reading.** `forkRunner` kill logic. The descendant list comes from one `ps` snapshot, so there is a theoretical pid-reuse window.\n   - Targets are only the worker's subtree plus its pgid, so it cannot reach pid 1, the server or the caller.\n   - If `ps` is missing, the verifier (which `timeout` moves out of the worker's process group) is orphaned until its deadline.\n4. **Non-blocking, documentation.** The MCP_GUIDE limit paragraph is honest but incomplete. It names `node_modules`, `.venv`, git config and the install. It omits:\n   - the server user's HOME: `~/.config/nexus/config.env` operator settings (for example `NEXUS_VERIFIER_ENV_PASSTHROUGH`) and any user-tier managed policy, which a same-user `run_command` can edit or delete;\n   - an operator verifier script that lives outside the workspace;\n   - PATH binaries;\n   - the SIGTERM and SIGKILL orphan behaviour.\n5. **Non-blocking, by reading.** The `readOnly` option is set only from `--read-only` or `--mode read-only`. `REDACTED` from the environment or config does not refuse `nexus_verify`. The doc says as much, but this is inconsistent with `loadConfig`.\n6. **Non-blocking, confirmed.** A failing verifier's `outputTail` in the receipt includes the server's passthrough environment (PATH, home paths, usernames, `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS` path). Secret-named variables were not passed, and the `receipt_sha256` matched the served bytes. The receipt also carries the real workspace path. This matches CLI output, and the caller already has `run_command env`.\n7. **Documented limit.** `node_modules` and `.venv` are linked live, so planting a shim there is outside the guard. I did not run this one.\n\n**Checked and held, all confirmed by running**\n- **Arguments, property 1.** Every shape was refused with -32602: object, array, string, null, number, false, empty string, nested, `__proto__`, `constructor`, `_meta` inside `arguments`. An extra `params` member was refused; `_meta` beside `arguments` and extra JSON-RPC envelope members were ignored and had no effect. Hostile `initialize` params and capabilities had no effect.\n- **Receipt URIs.** Traversal, a trailing slash, a query string, upper case, a 100 KB id, a newline, `file://`, percent-escapes and other receipt ids all returned \"not available\".\n- **Pinned base, property 3:**\n  - Weakened test committed plus `update-ref`: BLOCKED / `VERIFICATION_EDIT_BLOCKED`.\n  - `git replace` of the base to the weakened commit: BLOCKED (`--no-replace-objects` is pinned).\n  - Amend plus gc pruning the base: usage error, no verdict.\n  - `core.worktree` repointed: usage error.\n  - `.git` replaced by a gitdir pointer: BLOCKED / `UNTRUSTED_CHECKOUT`.\n  - A `filter.clean` command plus `.gitattributes`: the filter never ran.\n  - `.git/info/exclude`: no raised verdict.\n- **Verification-path tampering, property 2.** Editing the operator's workspace `verify.sh` gave `VERIFIER_DEFINITION_CHANGED`. A `.npmrc` `script-shell` gave the same. Rewriting the `package.json` test script gave `VERIFICATION_EDIT_BLOCKED`. With no operator verifier, nothing ran (BLOCKED / `VERIFIER_NOT_APPROVED`; covered by the PR test and mutation M2, not re-driven by hand).\n- **Worker path, property 7.** By reading, it comes from `import.meta.url` and `import.meta.resolve(\"tsx\")`, not from cwd, env or PATH. The verifier's stdio has no IPC fd.\n\n**Mutations (each reverted)**\n\n| Mutation | Test run | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Accept an argument silently | `mcp_verify` acceptance | fail |\n| Allow a discovered verifier | `mcp_verify` discovered-verifier test | fail |\n| Drop the base pin | `verify_service` pinned-base test | fail (VERIFIED instead of BLOCKED) |\n| Skip the kill | `verify_service` watchdog test | fail (orphans appeared) |\n\n**Tests at head, one at a time**\n- `verify_service`: 10/10\n- `mcp_verify`: 6/6\n- `mcp`: 4/4\n- `verify_hostile`: 46/46\n- `verify_command`: 18/18\n\n**Not checked**\n- ACP, which is slice 2 and not in this PR.\n- `.gitattributes` attributes such as `eol` and `working-tree-encoding` affecting how `checkout-index` writes the base and head trees.\n- Docker as the sandbox engine.\n- Node 22 execArgv behaviour (I ran Node 24 only).\n- A real `node_modules` tamper.\n- The ordering of a concurrent `run_command` against an in-flight verify.\n- A hard SIGKILL of the real server pid. My SIGKILL run in direct mode showed orphans, but I did not time the verifier's eventual expiry.\n\nThe worktree is removed, no commits or pushes were made, and no processes I started remain.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>151694</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>43</tool_uses><duration_ms>1498922</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
