{"parentUuid":"991d2b0f-b4be-44d4-b608-7cde26f2aace","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"be7f6299-a82c-4ba4-8ced-6aa0e3072e64","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ada00b9304ff81b6e</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01RADtJ5h7AjNXxnHxwYUDnx</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/ada00b9304ff81b6e.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Third review PR #758\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS** at `9b637c48addb2fc788d6b58afa9919d86dd62a32`. The PR head did not move by the end of the review. I did not run `tsc`; its output was cut off in the background run.\n\n**Blocker (fixed).** `operation_entry_integrity.test.ts` now passes 3 of 3. `pauseForStaleDriver` in `cli/src/core/operation_runner.ts` returns early for a RUNNING operation whose units are all `WAITING_ON_DEPENDENCY` with `runs === 0` and no `agentTaskId`. I ran the real `driveOperation` against seeded state:\n- A fresh plan with no identity is not paused.\n- A started unit (`RUNNABLE` with `runs=1`) with no identity comes back PAUSED.\n- A started unit with a dead pid comes back PAUSED.\n\n**Original restart protection.** It still holds at goal level. I probed `goalStatus` with seeded state across 6 statuses and 7 identity shapes:\n- RUNNING and WAITING with no identity, a dead pid, or an old boot come back PAUSED.\n- RUNNING, WAITING and BLOCKED with a live pid stay as they were.\n- An old boot id with a live pid is paused, which is correct.\n- PAUSED, COMPLETE and CANCELLED are untouched.\n\nBLOCKED is no longer treated as stale in `driveOperation`. A BLOCKED operation with a dead pid and a blocked unit stays BLOCKED on resume (probed).\n\n**Non-blocking items.**\n- Doc limits: partly done. The doc now describes the BLOCKED and fresh-plan behaviour. It still doesn't state the three limits: a reused pid reads as alive; a state from another host, or with a pid and no host, stays RUNNING; boot id only. My probe confirms the last two behaviours.\n- `pauseReason ?? stale` precedence: unchanged. A stale explicit-pause reason wins, which is acceptable.\n- Reason text: unchanged. The goal and operation wording is duplicated across `goal.ts` and `operation_runner.ts`. This is cosmetic.\n- Host-boot test early `return` without `/proc`: not fixed. The test silently passes when there is no boot id.\n- `(state as any)` casts: still present in `operation_runner.ts`.\n\n**New findings (none blocking).**\n1. There is no test for the `driveOperation` stale path. Nothing in the tests mentions `pauseForStaleDriver`, `stale_driver` or `resumeAfterDecision`, and the only test change is `cli/test/goal_lifecycle.test.ts`. Both new tests there use fresh plans, which the new early return skips, so only the goal-level check is exercised. Fix: add a test with a started unit (`runs=1`, dead pid) driven through `driveOperation` with `entry: \"existing\"`, asserting PAUSED with the \"Paused on resume\" text. Add a second test for the `resumeAfterDecision` handoff.\n2. The goal and operation layers disagree. `goalStatus` reports PAUSED for BLOCKED with a dead pid and for a never-started plan with no identity. `driveOperation` leaves both alone. The new doc sentence that a BLOCKED operation \"remains at that decision boundary\" is true only for `driveOperation`. Fix: align the two checks, or word the doc to match.\n3. `resumeAfterDecision` stamps the current pid before the stale check. It is passed only by `operation decide`, so I saw no way for model text, a tool call or workspace content to set it. I did not test whether a concurrent driver could be affected.\n\n**Mutations.**\n- Goal-level stale check removed: `goal_lifecycle` fails 2 of 7, as required.\n- `driveOperation` stale call removed: `goal_lifecycle`, `operation_entry_integrity`, `operation_runner_restart`, `operation_lock`, `operation_concurrency` and `operation_runner_refactor` all still pass. This is finding 1. Both mutations were restored with `git checkout`.\n\n**Policy and lifecycle.** Lifecycle changes stay inside the goal and operation drivers, and `cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts` is not in the PR's changed files, so approval, budget and managed-policy checks are not loosened. I read the diff for any path where model text, a tool call or workspace content could change a goal's lifecycle and found none.\n\n**Test counts at head** (one file at a time, throwaway HOME, no failures):\n\n| Test file | Pass |\n|---|---|\n| `goal_ledger_propagation` | 2 |\n| `goal_lifecycle` | 7 |\n| `operation_account_identity` | 14 |\n| `operation_concurrency` | 16 |\n| `operation_entry_integrity` | 3 |\n| `operation_git` | 42 |\n| `operation_lock` | 9 |\n| `operation_messages` | 8 |\n| `operation_release_acceptance` | 4 |\n| `operation_release_review` | 24 |\n| `operation_runner_execution_diagnostics` | 3 |\n| `operation_runner_refactor` | 3 |\n| `operation_runner_restart` | 1 |\n| `operation_schema` | 18 |\n| `operation_view` | 34 |\n| `leak_guard` | 6 |\n\nThe delta adds no new test files, so there was nothing new to register with the leak guard.\n\n**Not checked.** `tsc`; CI status on the PR; the loop and batch resume callers beyond reading `driveOperation`; real-process crash behaviour (probes used seeded state); Node 22 versus 24. The worktree `rev758c` is removed.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>48296</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>19</tool_uses><duration_ms>337086</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"},"uuid":"d371c2e7-c343-4f89-bdd0-5671493ffc07","timestamp":"2026-11-24T00:49:18.650Z","permissionMode":"bypassPermissions","origin":{"kind":"task-notification","producer":"session-task","runId":"0mwtz1iqe-f75545e6"},"promptSource":"system