{"parentUuid":"d9cf199f-4ab2-46b6-aa66-ee79f672294c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMDkvRRinV8igJQQRKcd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both extra suites pass on tip 5abbc4cb. PR #771 is now out for independent review.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":832,"cache_read_input_tokens":670257,"output_tokens":2653,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":832,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2653,"cache_read_input_tokens":670257,"cache_creation_input_tokens":832,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":832},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":7,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011ChMDkv37tYgV9t8nNW9kp","requestRef":"fd5f5924-06f4-41ce-802d-7aa522af4557","requestedModel":"claude-fable-5-2","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"d187d832-54f2-414c-9ab7-295817e327bc","timestamp":"2026-11-24T05:43:33.423Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","madeBy":"model","session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"d187d832-54f2-414c-9ab7-295817e327bc","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMDkvRRinV8igJQQRKcd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LKorFqbfAV5TN1RfdJJp6h","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Review PR #771 audit candidate binding","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent pre-merge review of GitHub PR #771 in the repo at ~/nexus-code (`gh pr view 771`, `gh pr diff 771`), \"feat(audit): bind exported receipts to the current candidate\", base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 771 --json headRefOid`, review exactly that, and check at the end whether it moved or was merged. Files: `cli/src/audit_export.ts`, `cli/test/audit_export.test.ts`, `docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md` (+160/−7).\n\nWhat it claims (read the PR body): `nexus audit export` adds `receipts/candidate-binding.json`, comparing each persisted validation receipt's candidate digest with a bounded computation of the recorded workspace's CURRENT candidate identity; statuses `matched`, `mismatch`, `partial`, `no_receipts`, `unavailable`; mismatches/unknowns are warnings in the human-readable export; the artifact says the comparison is a separate, non-atomic sample and does not establish verifier success or task acceptance; reuses the existing candidate-identity and checkout-trust routines; scan bounded to 50,000 entries and 256 MiB, report to 10,000 receipts; unsupported or untrusted checkouts → `unavailable`.\n\nContext: audit bundles (GV-2: `cli/src/audit_export.ts`, `cli/src/core/audit_bundle.ts`, `cli/src/audit_contract.ts`, `docs/AUDIT.md`, schemas and ledgers under `docs/protocol/`, tests `cli/test/audit_{export,bundle,command,schema}.test.ts`). A bundle has a manifest with file hashes; `nexus audit verify` checks it (\"one changed byte fails and names the file\"). Candidate identity / verification receipts come from the VR-1 work (`cli/src/core/verify*.ts`, hardened in PRs #732/#737 against forged evidence).\n\nAnswer with file:line, by running where practical:\n1. Can the new report mislead? (a) Could `matched` be produced for a workspace whose content differs from what was validated (which inputs go into the candidate digest: tracked files only? untracked? ignored? symlinks, submodules, file modes; does it use the same routine the receipt was produced with, at the same settings, so equal content always gives equal digests and unequal content never does)? (b) Could a hostile workspace (the export reads the workspace recorded in state — an attacker-influenced directory) make the export hang, exhaust memory, follow symlinks outside the workspace, run a git hook/filter/`core.fsmonitor`/textconv, or execute anything (how is git invoked: with the hardened flags the verify code uses? `GIT_DIR` handling? does the \"checkout-trust routine\" refuse before any git command that could run configured programs)? (c) Are the bounds enforced before reading (50,000 entries / 256 MiB / 10,000 receipts): what is reported when a bound is hit — it must not be `matched`. (d) Does `partial`/`unavailable`/`mismatch` ever become exit status 0 with no visible warning? What is the exit status policy for `audit export` on mismatch (the PR says warning) and is that consistent with how other export warnings behave?\n2. Bundle integrity: is `receipts/candidate-binding.json` covered by the manifest hashes like every other file, so `audit verify` detects tampering with it (try: export, flip a byte in that file, verify → must fail and name the file)? Does adding a file change the bundle/manifest schema or need a ledger/schema-version entry (`docs/protocol/*AUDIT*`, `cli/test/audit_schema.test.ts`), and does an older `audit verify` (before this PR) accept a bundle with the extra file, and the new verify accept an older bundle without it? Is the report deterministic for a fixed input (ordering, timestamps) — if it embeds a timestamp or absolute paths, is that intended and documented; does it leak host paths or content beyond what the bundle already contains?\n3. Privacy/redaction: does the report include file names or digests only, or any file content? Does it pass through the same redaction the rest of the export uses?\n4. Tests: do they fail if (a) a mismatch is reported as matched, (b) the bounds are removed, (c) the untrusted-checkout refusal is removed? Mutate (then `git checkout`) and run `cli/test/audit_export.test.ts`. Run at head, one file at a time: `audit_export`, `audit_bundle`, `audit_command`, `audit_schema`, `leak_guard`, `verify_hostile`, `check_requirements`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`. For any failure check the same file on `origin/release/v1.3.0`.\n5. Complexity gate: this repo now fails CI if listed critical functions grow (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`, `cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`, run by `make lint`). Does the PR touch a listed function? (If `@babel/parser` is missing from the copied node_modules, say you could not run the gate.)\n6. Docs: does the plan text, and `docs/AUDIT.md` if it should mention the new file (does it? should it?), claim only what code and tests show?\n\nMandatory rules:\n- Do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev771 <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev771/cli/`; copy `~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui}` into `~/nexus-wt/rev771/core/target/release/`. Remove the worktree at the end (`git -C ~/nexus-code worktree remove --force ~/nexus-wt/rev771`). No commits, pushes or GitHub comments.\n- Every run uses a throwaway HOME: `cd cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`, foreground, one file at a time, timeout at most 9 minutes (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`). Probe scripts live in a separate scratch directory. Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus. No live inference, no network.\n- Stop every process you start by its own pid; never `pkill -f`. No background loops. Aim for about 35 minutes.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); output passes through `rtk` (use `rtk proxy <cmd>` if it looks filtered).\n\nReport under 550 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact full SHA; findings ranked with file:line, each marked confirmed-by-running or by-reading, blocking vs non-blocking; mutation results; test counts; what you did not check."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":832,"cache_read_input_tokens":670257,"output_tokens":2653,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":832,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2653,"cache_read_input_tokens":670257,"cache_creation_input_tokens":832,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":832},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":22119,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01LKorFqbfAV5TN1RfdJJp6h":{"description":"Review PR #771 audit candidate binding","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent pre-merge review of GitHub PR #771 in the repo at ~/nexus-code (`gh pr view 771`, `gh pr diff 771`), \"feat(audit): bind exported receipts to the current candidate\", base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 771 --json headRefOid`, review exactly that, and check at the end whether it moved or was merged. Files: `cli/src/audit_export.ts`, `cli/test/audit_export.test.ts`, `docs/assurance/POWER_OF_TEN_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md` (+160/−7).\n\nWhat it claims (read the PR body): `nexus audit export` adds `receipts/candidate-binding.json`, comparing each persisted validation receipt's candidate digest with a bounded computation of the recorded workspace's CURRENT candidate identity; statuses `matched`, `mismatch`, `partial`, `no_receipts`, `unavailable`; mismatches/unknowns are warnings in the human-readable export; the artifact says the comparison is a separate, non-atomic sample and does not establish verifier success or task acceptance; reuses the existing candidate-identity and checkout-trust routines; scan bounded to 50,000 entries and 256 MiB, report to 10,000 receipts; unsupported or untrusted checkouts → `unavailable`.\n\nContext: audit bundles (GV-2: `cli/src/audit_export.ts`, `cli/src/core/audit_bundle.ts`, `cli/src/audit_contract.ts`, `docs/AUDIT.md`, schemas and ledgers under `docs/protocol/`, tests `cli/test/audit_{export,bundle,command,schema}.test.ts`). A bundle has a manifest with file hashes; `nexus audit verify` checks it (\"one changed byte fails and names the file\"). Candidate identity / verification receipts come from the VR-1 work (`cli/src/core/verify*.ts`, hardened in PRs #732/#737 against forged evidence).\n\nAnswer with file:line, by running where practical:\n1. Can the new report mislead? (a) Could `matched` be produced for a workspace whose content differs from what was validated (which inputs go into the candidate digest: tracked files only? untracked? ignored? symlinks, submodules, file modes; does it use the same routine the receipt was produced with, at the same settings, so equal content always gives equal digests and unequal content never does)? (b) Could a hostile workspace (the export reads the workspace recorded in state — an attacker-influenced directory) make the export hang, exhaust memory, follow symlinks outside the workspace, run a git hook/filter/`core.fsmonitor`/textconv, or execute anything (how is git invoked: with the hardened flags the verify code uses? `GIT_DIR` handling? does the \"checkout-trust routine\" refuse before any git command that could run configured programs)? (c) Are the bounds enforced before reading (50,000 entries / 256 MiB / 10,000 receipts): what is reported when a bound is hit — it must not be `matched`. (d) Does `partial`/`unavailable`/`mismatch` ever become exit status 0 with no visible warning? What is the exit status policy for `audit export` on mismatch (the PR says warning) and is that consistent with how other export warnings behave?\n2. Bundle integrity: is `receipts/candidate-binding.json` covered by the manifest hashes like every other file, so `audit verify` detects tampering with it (try: export, flip a byte in that file, verify → must fail and name the file)? Does adding a file change the bundle/manifest schema or need a ledger/schema-version entry (`docs/protocol/*AUDIT*`, `cli/test/audit_schema.test.ts`), and does an older `audit verify` (before this PR) accept a bundle with the extra file, and the new verify accept an older bundle without it? Is the report deterministic for a fixed input (ordering, timestamps) — if it embeds a timestamp or absolute paths, is that intended and documented; does it leak host paths or content beyond what the bundle already contains?\n3. Privacy/redaction: does the report include file names or digests only, or any file content? Does it pass through the same redaction the rest of the export uses?\n4. Tests: do they fail if (a) a mismatch is reported as matched, (b) the bounds are removed, (c) the untrusted-checkout refusal is removed? Mutate (then `git checkout`) and run `cli/test/audit_export.test.ts`. Run at head, one file at a time: `audit_export`, `audit_bundle`, `audit_command`, `audit_schema`, `leak_guard`, `verify_hostile`, `check_requirements`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`. For any failure check the same file on `origin/release/v1.3.0`.\n5. Complexity gate: this repo now fails CI if listed critical functions grow (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`, `cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`, run by `make lint`). Does the PR touch a listed function? (If `@babel/parser` is missing from the copied node_modules, say you could not run the gate.)\n6. Docs: does the plan text, and `docs/AUDIT.md` if it should mention the new file (does it? should it?), claim only what code and tests show?\n\nMandatory rules:\n- Do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev771 <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev771/cli/`; copy `~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui}` into `~/nexus-wt/rev771/core/target/release/`. Remove the worktree at the end (`git -C ~/nexus-code worktree remove --force ~/nexus-wt/rev771`). No commits, pushes or GitHub comments.\n- Every run uses a throwaway HOME: `cd cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`, foreground, one file at a time, timeout at most 9 minutes (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`). Probe scripts live in a separate scratch directory. Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus. No live inference, no network.\n- Stop every process you start by its own pid; never `pkill -f`. No background loops. Aim for about 35 minutes.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); output passes through `rtk` (use `rtk proxy <cmd>` if it looks filtered).\n\nReport under 550 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact full SHA; findings ranked with file:line, each marked confirmed-by-running or by-reading, blocking vs non-blocking; mutation results; test counts; what you did not check."}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011ChMDkv37tYgV9t8nNW9kp","requestRef":"fd5f5924-06f4-41ce-802d-7aa522af4557","requestedMode