{"parentUuid":"70b44e8e-d45a-42c8-a637-93c38cc30b60","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMFAyRJMbhv3A2M2uLqW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#771 has a new head (`64a3b053`), presumably the fix for the git-filter exposure. I'm sending it for a re-review that reruns the exploit."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":878,"cache_read_input_tokens":680095,"output_tokens":2396,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":878,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2396,"cache_read_input_tokens":680095,"cache_creation_input_tokens":878,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":878},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011ChMFAy3jJLWKRRx1QRQvX","requestRef":"3a75e188-738a-43db-b215-0f194def9f5f","requestedModel":"claude-fable-5-2","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"75fc3597-179c-466b-b635-df225e644c4c","timestamp":"2026-11-24T06:02:05.630Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","madeBy":"model","session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"75fc3597-179c-466b-b635-df225e644c4c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMFAyRJMbhv3A2M2uLqW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011kta5PLogADoN1rmx6jLqT","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Re-review PR #771 new head","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Re-review GitHub PR #771 in the repo at ~/nexus-code (`gh pr view 771 --comments`, `gh pr diff 771`), \"feat(audit): bind exported receipts to the current candidate\", base `release/v1.3.0`. The previous review (the comment beginning \"Independent review at `7e58def9…`\") requested changes. The author pushed a new head (short `64a3b053`; take the full SHA from `gh pr view 771 --json headRefOid`, review exactly that, and check at the end whether it moved or was merged). See `git diff 7e58def9..<new> -- cli docs` for what changed.\n\nVerify by running:\n1. BLOCKER: `nexus audit export` computed the candidate identity by running git (`git status` etc. via `computeCandidateIdentitySync` in `cli/src/core/candidate.ts`, called from `cli/src/audit_export.ts` ~646) in the workspace named by recorded state, with only `core.fsmonitor=false` and `core.hooksPath=/dev/null`, so a clean filter configured in that repository executed. Reproduce the exploit exactly as before against the new head: a throwaway git repo with `.gitattributes` containing `* filter=x`, `.git/config` with `[filter \"x\"] clean = <command that writes a marker file>` (also try `smudge`, `process`, a `diff.x.textconv` + `* diff=x`, `core.fsmonitor=<cmd>`, `core.sshCommand`, `core.pager`, `core.editor`, a `post-index-change`/`reference-transaction`/`pre-auto-gc` hook, `include.path`/`includeIf` pointing at a file that sets a filter, an alias, `core.attributesFile` and `.git/info/attributes` setting the filter, a global-looking config in the workspace, `safe.directory` tricks, a `.git` FILE pointing at a hostile gitdir elsewhere, a repo with `core.worktree` set, a submodule with a filter, a bare-repo-in-subdirectory), touch a tracked file so git must re-hash it, then drive BOTH the function the export calls and the real export end to end (`nexus audit export` through the CLI entry with a throwaway HOME and seeded state, as `cli/test/audit_export.test.ts` does) and check whether any marker file appears. For each vector report: executed / not executed / export refused (`unavailable`). The claim to test is that NO configured program from the workspace runs during an audit export.\n   Also check the fix does not break the legitimate case: a normal repo with a validation receipt still reports `matched`, and after an edit `mismatch`; and that if filters are simply ignored, the digest is still computed the same way as when the receipt was produced (otherwise a repo that legitimately uses a filter, e.g. git-lfs or CRLF conversion via `text=auto`/`eol`, would report a false `mismatch`: say what happens for `* text=auto` with a CRLF file, and for a repo with a real filter — `unavailable` is acceptable, a false `matched` is not, a false `mismatch` should be documented).\n   Did the fix change the shared routine (so receipts are also produced with filters disabled — check every caller of the candidate-identity code and whether existing receipts' digests would now differ from before, which would make every pre-existing receipt mismatch) or only the export path?\n2. Tests: are there tests for the hostile cases (at least the clean filter) that fail if the hardening is removed? Mutate (then `git checkout`) and run. Earlier gaps: no test for the 50,000-entry / 256 MiB bounds, none with an untrusted checkout.\n3. Status of the earlier non-blocking items: receipts computed on the active worktree path vs the recorded workspace path (`cli/src/core/spine_handlers.ts` ~452) — same directory?; the `no_receipts` warning on every receipt-less export; exit-status policy stated; `docs/AUDIT.md` updated (file list ~line 70, \"the two `receipts/` files\" ~line 115).\n4. Run at head, one file at a time: `audit_export`, `audit_command`, `audit_bundle`, `audit_schema`, `leak_guard`, `verify_hostile`, `verify_command`, `check_requirements`, any test file the delta adds; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`. For any failure check the same file on `origin/release/v1.3.0`.\n\nMandatory rules:\n- Do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev771b <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev771b/cli/`; copy `~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui}` into `~/nexus-wt/rev771b/core/target/release/`. Remove the worktree at the end (`git -C ~/nexus-code worktree remove --force ~/nexus-wt/rev771b`). No commits, pushes or GitHub comments.\n- All hostile repos live under a fresh `mktemp -d` directory; marker files are written only inside it; the \"commands\" are harmless (`touch <marker>` / `sh -c 'echo x > marker'`). Every run uses a throwaway HOME: `cd cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`, foreground, one file at a time, timeout at most 9 minutes (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`). Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus. No live inference, no network.\n- Stop every process you start by its own pid; never `pkill -f`. No background loops. Aim for about 40 minutes; an experiment that fails to converge twice is \"not confirmed\".\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); output passes through `rtk` (use `rtk proxy <cmd>` if it looks filtered).\n\nReport under 550 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact full SHA; a table of vectors with executed / not executed / refused; whether the shared routine changed and what that does to existing receipts; the legitimate-case results; new findings ranked, blocking vs not; mutation results; test counts; what you did not check."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":878,"cache_read_input_tokens":680095,"output_tokens":2396,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":878,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2396,"cache_read_input_tokens":680095,"cache_creation_input_tokens":878,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":878},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":21176,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_011kta5PLogADoN1rmx6jLqT":{"description":"Re-review PR #771 new head","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Re-review GitHub PR #771 in the repo at ~/nexus-code (`gh pr view 771 --comments`, `gh pr diff 771`), \"feat(audit): bind exported receipts to the current candidate\", base `release/v1.3.0`. The previous review (the comment beginning \"Independent review at `7e58def9…`\") requested changes. The author pushed a new head (short `64a3b053`; take the full SHA from `gh pr view 771 --json headRefOid`, review exactly that, and check at the end whether it moved or was merged). See `git diff 7e58def9..<new> -- cli docs` for what changed.\n\nVerify by running:\n1. BLOCKER: `nexus audit export` computed the candidate identity by running git (`git status` etc. via `computeCandidateIdentitySync` in `cli/src/core/candidate.ts`, called from `cli/src/audit_export.ts` ~646) in the workspace named by recorded state, with only `core.fsmonitor=false` and `core.hooksPath=/dev/null`, so a clean filter configured in that repository executed. Reproduce the exploit exactly as before against the new 