nNothing in the shared routine (`candidate.ts`) changes, so receipt digests are exactly what they were.\n\n## Trade-off\n\nA repository that legitimately defines a filter in its own `.git/config` (for example `git lfs install --local`) now gets `unavailable` from the binding instead of a comparison. That is the conservative answer and it is visible in the report. A filter configured at the operator's global level is still honoured, because that configuration is the operator's to trust.\n\n## Tests\n\nNew test in `audit_export.test.ts` with five planted configurations (a clean filter; a filter named only in `.git/info/attributes`; a filter defined in an included file; a file-system monitor; an attributes file named by the repository). For each: the planted command's marker file must not exist after the export, and the binding must be `unavailable` with the new reason. With the check disabled the test fails.\n\nLocally on Node 24: `audit_export` 20/20, `audit_command` 8/8, `audit_bundle` 25/25, `audit_schema` 7/7, `leak_guard` 6/6, `tsc --noEmit` clean.\n\n## Not done here\n\n- The same exposure exists for every other caller of the candidate-identity routine (`agent.ts`, `spine_handlers.ts`, `worktree.ts`, `state.ts`, `operation_runner_execution.ts`). Those run in the operator's own session on the operator's workspace, which is a different trust situation; it deserves its own decision.\n- Not covered by the new test: `includeIf`, a `.git` file pointing elsewhere, `core.worktree`, a process filter, a registered submodule. The key list covers the first four by reading; a submodule's own configuration is not listed by this check.\n- The other open items from the review of #771 (the `no_receipts` warning, `docs/AUDIT.md`, the worktree path question, tests for the scan bounds).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011ChMHAmCpo89JtT4qxLvsM","requestRef":"4e415f67-0099-4042-bd31-16962c55234c","requestedMode