{"parentUuid":"0b18d2a1-d7d1-4943-a819-2fff8b2b1dc3","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMHCa8N31iU7xAmsqu5s","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The fix for #771's blocker is up as #772, a PR into Codex's #771 branch. I did not push to that branch.\n\n- **What it does:** before the audit export computes a candidate identity, it lists the repository's own git configuration (including what it includes) for keys that name a program git runs or that redirect where git looks. Any hit makes the binding \"unavailable\" with a specific reason. The shared candidate routine is untouched, so receipt digests don't change.\n- **Tested:** a new test plants five of the hostile configurations and asserts that the planted command never runs and the binding is unavailable. It fails with the check disabled. All five audit test files pass locally on Node 24.\n- **Trade-off:** a repository that legitimately defines a filter in its own git config (for example a local git-lfs install) now gets \"unavailable\" from the binding instead of a comparison. A filter set in the operator's global config is still honoured.\n- **Not covered by #772:**\n  - the same exposure for the other callers of the candidate routine, which run in the operator's own session and need their own decision;\n  - four of the nine vectors, which the key list covers by reading but the test does not exercise;\n  - a submodule's own configuration.\n\n#771 itself is still open with changes requested; its newest commit again only changes test expectations. I've noted in the claims log that it should not merge without #772 or an equivalent.\n\nThe release branch is unchanged and green at `5abbc4cb`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2265,"cache_read_input_tokens":711512,"output_tokens":465,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_u