{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","hash":"0c56326f4310264622b63477f79cc6dff0bafcd29d2d556c085f47528df7cad3","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0164GS4frTZ8fbwrrxH3wQDB","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Implement VR-2 follow-ups","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Implement the six open review follow-ups of unit VR-2 in the Nexus Code CLI. Work ONLY in the git worktree `/home/mike/nexus-wt/vr2f` (branch `v1/vr2-followups`, cut from `origin/release/v1.3.0`). Do not commit, push, open PRs, or touch any other worktree or `~/nexus-code` itself. Do not ask questions; where a detail is open choose the conservative option and list it in your report.\n\nBackground: `nexus acp` (Agent Client Protocol server over stdio for an UNTRUSTED client; `cli/src/acp_stdio.ts`, `cli/src/acp/{types,validate,mapping}.ts`, schema `docs/protocol/acp-adapter-v*.schema.json` generated by `cli/scripts/gen_acp_schema.mjs`, ledger `docs/protocol/ACP_ADAPTER_VERSIONS.json`, docs `docs/ACP.md`, evidence `docs/evidence/v1.3.0/VR2.md`) has an extension method `_nexus/verify` that calls the shared `VerifyService` (`cli/src/core/verify_service.ts`) and returns a verdict and receipt id; receipts are read with `_nexus/receipt/get`. Design plan: `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/VR2_PLAN.md` (sections 2, 3 and \"Decisions taken\"). Read the ACP verify code first (`grep -n \"_nexus/verify\\|latestReceipts\\|pinBase\\|verify\" cli/src/acp_stdio.ts`).\n\nFix these, each with tests:\n\n1. Base pin per workspace, not per session. Today the base commit is pinned in `startSession` for every new or loaded session, so a client can commit weakened tests, open a NEW session on the same directory, and be judged against a base that already contains them. Pin once per canonical workspace path for the lifetime of the `nexus acp` process: the first session on a directory pins `HEAD`; every later session (new or load) on that same canonical directory reuses that pin. The map must not grow without bound from client-chosen cwds: cap it (a small multiple of `ACP_MAX_SESSIONS`); beyond the cap, `_nexus/verify` for a not-yet-pinned workspace is refused with a clear error — never silently re-pin or evict a pin. Test: session A on dir D pins B0; make a commit in D; session B on D still uses B0 (assert via the base passed to the service / the receipt's base).\n\n2. The `_nexus/verify` result must name what was covered: add the canonical workspace path and the pinned base commit to the result summary (the receipt already carries `workspace` and `base_pinned_at`). Update types, validation/mapping, regenerate the ACP schema with the generator and make its `--check` mode pass; follow the repo's versioning rule for the ACP adapter schema (look at how v2 and v3 were added in `ACP_ADAPTER_VERSIONS.json` and `cli/test/acp_schema.test.ts`: earlier versions' files must stay byte-identical; add a new version if the result shape changes).\n\n3. Id-less `_nexus/receipt/get` must return TURN receipts only (plan decision 7: existing clients see no change). Today `latestReceipts` mixes verification receipts in, and a test asserts that. Keep verification receipts readable only by explicit id, bound to the session. Update the test: after a `_nexus/verify`, id-less get still returns the last turn receipt (or the existing \"not available\" error when there is none) and get-by-id returns the verification receipt.\n\n4. Interlock tests (a mutation showed they are untested): `_nexus/verify` while a prompt is running on that session is refused and nothing runs; `_nexus/verify` while the session is `stopping` is refused; `session/prompt` while a verification is running is refused; `_nexus/verify` in read-only mode is refused with no receipt; a cross-session receipt read uses a SECOND REAL ACTIVE session on the same connection and must fail. Confirm by mutation that removing the verify-during-prompt check fails a test (copy the source file aside and restore it from the copy — do not use `git checkout` to undo a mutation while you have uncommitted work).\n\n5. Shutdown bound: `ACP_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_MS` (10 s) equals the verify kill grace, so on a signal the process can exit before a running verification's worker has cleaned up. While a verification is running, the signal deadline must be at least the verify kill grace + 5 s (see what `McpServer.startStdio` in `cli/src/mcp.ts` does). Keep the existing stop semantics in `runAcpStdio`; only lengthen the bound when a verification is in flight. Add a test if cheap (there is a `shutdownGraceMs` option and existing signal tests in `cli/test/acp_review_fixes.test.ts`); otherwise say it is untested.\n\n6. `_meta` on `_nexus/verify`: make it consistent with how the adapter treats `_meta` on its other methods (check `cli/src/acp/validate.ts`) and state the behaviour in `docs/ACP.md`.\n\nDocs, honestly, in `docs/ACP.md` (the `_nexus/verify` section) and `docs/evidence/v1.3.0/VR2.md`: the client chooses the directory a session works in, so a verification covers that directory; the operator's verifier command and deliverable list are fixed at start-up and applied to the session's directory (read `cli/src/config.ts` near `normalizeTrustedDeliverableFiles` and the worker to describe exactly how deliverables resolve); the result and receipt name the workspace and base; the base is pinned per workspace for the life of the process, so commits made before the first session on that directory are the baseline and restarting `nexus acp` re-pins; the same-user limit described in `docs/MCP_GUIDE.md` applies. In the evidence doc replace any test count you cannot reproduce with counts from your own run.\n\nWorking rules (mandatory):\n- Tests: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/vr2f/cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`), one file at a time, foreground. Never read or write the real `~/.nexus`, `/etc/nexus`, `~/.config/nexus`. No live inference, no network.\n- `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .` must be clean. `cli/node_modules` is a symlink; do not run npm install or cargo.\n- New test files import `./helpers/leak_guard.js` first; clear every timer and stop every child you start by its own pid (never `pkill`).\n- Run at the end, one at a time: `acp_stdio`, `acp_validate`, `acp_schema`, `acp_mapping`, `acp_review_fixes`, `acp_client_conformance`, `acp_jsonrpc`, `verify_service`, `leak_guard`, any new test file; `node cli/scripts/gen_acp_schema.mjs --check` (or its actual check invocation); `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`; `node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts` from the repo root (a CI gate: listed critical functions must not grow in complexity). Run `acp_stdio` three times in a row to check for flakes.\n- The recorded real-client fixtures in `cli/test/fixtures/acp/clients/` must keep replaying (`acp_client_conformance`).\n- Preserve existing comments and unrelated code. Comments explain why, briefly. Minimum code that meets the spec.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c '...'` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); a hook filters output through `rtk` (prefix with `rtk proxy` if grep/sed output looks odd).\n\nReport (under 450 words): per item: file:line of the change, the tests added, and anything left open; exact test results per file; the mutation result; gates run; deviations and why."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01284R4pgJy7JFABQT3TK9xb","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Implement GV-1 slices 3-4 and GV-5","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Finish units GV-1 (slices 3 and 4) and GV-5 (MCP and plugin allowlists) of the Nexus Code v1.3.0 plan. Work ONLY in the git worktree `/home/mike/nexus-wt/gv1c` (branch `v1/gv1-slices-3-4`, cut from `origin/release/v1.3.0`). Do not commit, push, open PRs, or touch any other worktree or `~/nexus-code` itself. Do not ask questions; where a detail is open choose the conservative (stricter, fail-closed) option and list it in your report.\n\nRead first, in this order: the unit rows in `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` (search \"GV-1\", \"GV-5\", gate \"G-3\", decision \"D16\"); the design plan `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/GV1_PLAN.md` (it defines the slices; slices 1 and 2 are merged); `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md` on this branch, which marks precisely what is \"not yet\" or \"planned\" — that list is your work list; the merged implementation `cli/src/core/managed_policy.ts` (library) and `cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts` (enforcement) and their tests (`cli/test/managed_policy*.test.ts`, `cli/test/policy_runtime*.test.ts` or similarly named: find them).\n\nScope (confirm each against `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md` and the plan; if the doc lists further \"not yet\"/\"planned\" items in the same area, include them or state why not):\nA. GV-5 allowlists: `mcp.allow`, `mcp.disable_workspace`, `plugins.allow`, `plugins.disable_workspace` are accepted by the schema and enforce nothing. Enforce them where MCP servers are configured/connected (`cli/src/mcp.ts`: `mcpConfigFiles`, the manager that loads servers) and where plugins are discovered/loaded (find the plugin loader). A server or plugin not on the allowlist is not started/loaded and is reported with the policy source; `disable_workspace` ignores workspace-level MCP/plugin definitions. A project/workspace policy can only tighten, never loosen, the system policy (the library already merges that way: reuse it). Fail closed on an unreadable policy.\nB. GV-1 slice 3: the TUI \"always approve\" answer under `approval.disable_auto` (documented as a gap: an \"always\" answer must not switch the session to auto when policy forbids it; see `cli/src/sidecar.ts` approval handling and the REPL in `cli/src/repl.ts`), and `sandbox.*` policy reaching `nexus verify --sandbox` (documented as \"does not reach … yet\").\nC. GV-1 slice 4: `nexus policy` commands (at least `nexus policy check` to validate and show the effective policy with the source of each value, and whatever else GV1_PLAN.md specifies for the CLI), and `nexus doctor` findings for policy (a configuration finding per GV1_PLAN.md; `nexus doctor --fix` must not modify a policy file).\nD. Anything `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md` marks \"not yet\" for `egress.secret_destinations` or similar: implement if GV1_PLAN.md puts it in slices 3–4; otherwise leave it marked and say so.\nThen update `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md` so every row says what is now true (no \"not yet\" for things you implemented; keep it for what you did not), the user manual/CLI reference if commands were added, and any ledger the repo requires for a new command or setting (`node scripts/check_requirements.mjs`, `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`, the doc-command check `scripts/doc_commands.mjs` used by the package smoke test — find what applies and make it pass).\n\nConstraints:\n- Other agents hold file claims on `cli/src/index.ts`, `cli/src/doctor.ts`, `cli/src/config.ts`, `cli/src/types.ts` for unrelated work. You may edit them, but keep each edit to a self-contained block (a new `program.command(\"policy\")` block; new doctor findings in their own function) and do not reformat or move existing code, so the merge is clean.\n- A CI gate fails if these functions grow in cyclomatic complexity or nesting: `runTurnImpl`, `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl`, `driveOperation` (see `cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`). If you need behaviour inside one of them, put the logic in a helper and call it without adding a branch there, and run the gate: from the repo root `node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`.\n- Tool descriptions offered to the model have a size budget (`cli/test/context_convergence.test.ts`, `cli/test/lsp_shape.test.ts`): do not add or lengthen model-facing tool descriptions.\n- Security posture: policy can only restrict; an invalid or unreadable system policy is exit 2 / refusal, never \"no policy\"; nothing a model, a tool result or a workspace file says may loosen it. Test the hostile cases: a workspace policy trying to add to an allowlist, a server name with odd characters or case differences matching an allowlist entry it should not, a symlinked workspace MCP config, an allowlist that is empty (allows nothing) versus absent (no restriction — check what the library defines and follow it).\n\nWorking rules (mandatory):\n- Tests: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/gv1c/cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`), one file at a time, foreground. A throwaway HOME is mandatory; never read or write the real `~/.nexus`, `/etc/nexus`, `~/.config/nexus` (tests point the policy loader at temp paths the way the existing policy tests do). No live inference, no network.\n- `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .` must be clean. `cli/node_modules` is a symlink; do not run npm install or cargo. If the TUI (Rust, `core/`) would need changes for item B, do NOT change Rust: implement the enforcement on the TypeScript sidecar side so the TUI cannot switch the mode, and report that the TUI text is unchanged.\n- New test files import `./helpers/leak_guard.js` first; clear timers and stop children by their own pid (never `pkill`). Stress new async tests: run each new file 3 times.\n- Run at the end, one at a time: every new or changed test file; the existing managed-policy and policy-runtime test files; `mcp.test.ts`, `mcp_verify.test.ts` (about 3 minutes), `verify_command`, `verify_sandbox`, `doctor.test.ts` (11 tests of it fail on this host on the base too — compare against `origin/release/v1.3.0` if you see failures and report only differences), `approval_policy`, `sidecar`, `context_convergence`, `check_requirements`, `leak_guard`, `release_workflow_parity`; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_requirements.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`; the complexity gate.\n- Preserve existing comments and unrelated code. Comments explain why, briefly. Minimum code that meets the spec; nothing speculative.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c '...'` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); a hook filters output through `rtk` (prefix with `rtk proxy` if grep/sed output looks odd).\n- If the work is too large to finish in one go, finish complete, tested pieces in this order: A (allowlists), C (policy check command + doctor finding), B (TUI always gap, sandbox to verify), D; and report exactly what is done and what is not. Never leave a half-wired setting that the docs would describe as enforced.\n\nReport (under 550 words): per item A–D: what is implemented, files and key line references, tests added (names), what is left; exact test results per file; gates run and their results; behaviour changes a release note must mention; deviations from GV1_PLAN.md and why."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WxAHM337b5m8R97TLcS9CA","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Plan BG-1, BG-2 and HK units","subagent_type":"architect","prompt":"Write implementation plans for five units of the Nexus Code v1.3.0 release: BG-1, BG-2 (background/unattended runs) and HK-1, HK-2, HK-3 (hooks and plugins). Write two files and nothing else: `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/BG_PLAN.md` and `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/HK_PLAN.md`. Do not edit code. Work read-only against `git -C ~/nexus-code show origin/release/v1.3.0:<path>` / `git -C ~/nexus-code grep <pattern> origin/release/v1.3.0 -- <paths>` (fetch first). Do not ask the user questions: where something is undecided take the plan's stated default, or else the conservative option, and record it under \"Decisions taken\".\n\nSources: `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` on that branch (read it all: unit rows BG-1, BG-2, BG-3, HK-1, HK-2, HK-3; gates G-3, G-4, G-6; decisions D15, D18, D19, D21 with their defaults; the phase ordering; the peer-project notes at the top). BG-3 is deferred to v1.4 by the plan: do not plan it. Existing plans for style and depth: `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/AC1_PLAN.md`, `VR2_PLAN.md`, `GV1_PLAN.md`. Existing building blocks to reuse (verify each by reading; cite file:line): the sidecar server and protocol (`cli/src/sidecar.ts`, `cli/src/protocol.ts`), `ApplicationCore` (`cli/src/core/app.ts`), `nexus run --json` and its contract (`cli/src/run_contract.ts`, `docs/protocol/RUN_RESULT_VERSIONS.json`), durable state and resume (`cli/src/core/state.ts`, session/task state), operations and goals (`cli/src/core/operation*.ts`, `cli/src/goal*.ts`, PA-1 resource admission), worktrees (`cli/src/worktree.ts` / worktrunk), approvals and modes (`cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts`, managed policy `cli/src/core/managed_policy.ts`: note `approval.strict`/`disable_auto`), verification (`cli/src/core/verify*.ts`, verification-path protection), the flight recorder and audit bundles, MCP and plugin loading (`cli/src/mcp.ts`, the plugin loader: find it), the execution spine and `dispatchToolCallImpl` (`cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts`, `cli/src/core/spine.ts`), the P10 budgets (invocation, inference submission, turn deadline), `nexus doctor`.\n\nHard constraints every plan must respect:\n- The approval gates, verification-path protection, managed policy and the VERIFIED outcome ladder are authorities no new surface may weaken. A hook's or plugin's \"allow\" must have no effect on a gate denial (HK-1 acceptance); an unattended run must never grant allow-always, must run in a fresh worktree, and a seeded failing verifier must end BLOCKED, not VERIFIED (BG-2/G-4).\n- A CI gate fails if `runTurnImpl`, `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl` or `driveOperation` grow in cyclomatic complexity or nesting (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`): new behaviour hooks in through a helper call that adds no branch there, or outside those functions.\n- Model-facing tool descriptions have a size budget (`cli/test/context_convergence.test.ts`): prefer no new model-facing tools.\n- Every new CLI command, setting, protocol message or file format needs: docs (G-6 wants a runnable example per new surface, run in CI), a schema/version ledger entry where the repo keeps one, tests that fail when the guard is removed, and the hostile cases for G-3 (client, hook, plugin, schedule, policy each trying to bypass a gate).\n- Tests run with a throwaway HOME, no network, no live inference (there is a scripted gateway fixture `cli/test/helpers/fixtures.ts` `startGateway`); new test files register `cli/test/helpers/leak_guard.ts`; child processes are stopped by pid.\n- Linux x86_64 is the only release target.\n\nFor EACH unit give: the acceptance criterion verbatim from the plan; what exists that it builds on (file:line); the design (data model, commands/flags, protocol or file formats with exact shapes, state on disk and its permissions, lifecycle, failure and crash behaviour, limits and timeouts); the security analysis (who is untrusted, what they control, each gate they could try to bypass and why they cannot); the exact list of files to add/change; the tests by name with what each proves, including the hostile ones and the kill/restart ones; docs and ledgers; and 2–4 mergeable slices in order, each independently green, with rough size (S/M/L) and the files it touches, flagging slices that touch `cli/src/index.ts`, `cli/src/doctor.ts`, `cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts`, `cli/src/core/run_turn.ts` or `cli/src/sidecar.ts` (hot files shared with other work). Keep each unit to what its acceptance needs: state explicitly what is out of scope for v1.3.0. Where two units share machinery (BG-1's detached runner and BG-2's unattended policy and schedule; HK-1's hook points and HK-2's plugin install/verify and HK-3's per-plugin trust), design it once and say which slice introduces it.\n\nSpecific questions the plans must answer:\n- BG-1 (\"kill the CLI; the run continues and resumes\"): what process owns a detached run (a supervisor? the sidecar re-parented? systemd-run --user? plain double-fork with a pidfile and a unix socket?), how a client attaches/detaches/lists/stops, where output and state live, how identity and liveness are established without pid-reuse mistakes (the repo just fixed several of those: record pid + kernel start time), what happens on machine reboot, how the P10 budgets and the turn deadline apply, and how it interacts with PF-3 (a unix-socket transport that is a separate unit: can BG-1 ship with attach-by-log-and-state only, leaving live attach to PF-3?).\n- BG-2 (\"seeded failing verifier ends BLOCKED; unattended approval denied\"): the unattended approval policy (default deny for anything that would prompt; no allow-always; `--yes`/auto refused or clamped per D19 and managed policy), fresh-worktree requirement, what \"schedule\" means in v1.3.0 (D21: event triggers and SSH execution — take the default; if scheduling proper is deferred, say exactly what small scheduling surface, if any, the acceptance needs), and the receipt/audit trail of an unattended run.\n- HK-1 (\"a hook's allow has no effect\"): which hook points exist (pre-tool, post-tool, on-stop? keep it minimal), how a hook is declared (operator config only? project config with trust?), what a hook may return (deny / annotate only — never allow), execution model (subprocess with a timeout, env, stdin JSON, sandboxed?), failure mode (hook error = deny or ignore? choose fail-closed for pre-tool), and how it attaches to dispatch without adding branches to `dispatchToolCallImpl`.\n- HK-2 (\"commit-pinned plugin verify\") and HK-3 (\"per-plugin trust\"): D18's default on in-process plugins; what a plugin is today in this repo (read the loader); install from a git URL pinned to a commit with a recorded digest, verify on load, refuse on mismatch; trust records per plugin (where stored, how approved, how revoked); how GV-5's `plugins.allow` / `plugins.disable_workspace` interact (another agent is implementing GV-5 enforcement now: say what you assume it provides).\n\nAlso record per plan: reference-coding note per `docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md` (what peer projects do — the plan's own notes at the top of PLAN.md cite some; do not spend long; \"XERJ unavailable: <reason>\" plus what you used is acceptable), risks, and open questions that could not be settled from the code.\n\nShell notes: zsh (use `bash -c` for loops); output is filtered by `rtk` (prefix `rtk proxy` when a grep/sed pipeline looks odd). Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus.\n\nReturn (under 600 words): the two file paths; for each unit the slice list with sizes and hot files; the decisions taken; the main risks; anything you could not determine. Be concrete enough that an implementer can start slice 1 of each unit from the plan alone."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FyFjrmv8VBGE8uDo2FKzzk","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Plan PF-3, PF-4, SB-1, MT-2, PA-2","subagent_type":"architect","prompt":"Write implementation plans for five units of the Nexus Code v1.3.0 release: PF-3, PF-4, SB-1, MT-2, PA-2. Write one file and nothing else: `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/PF_SB_MT_PA_PLAN.md` (one top-level section per unit). Do not edit code. Work read-only against `git -C ~/nexus-code show origin/release/v1.3.0:<path>` / `git -C ~/nexus-code grep <pattern> origin/release/v1.3.0 -- <paths>` (fetch first). Do not ask the user questions: where something is undecided take the plan's stated default, or else the conservative option, and record it under \"Decisions taken\".\n\nSources: `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md` on that branch (read it all: the unit rows; gates G-1..G-7; decisions D13 (npm publish: default \"no publish; the entry point is documented but not published\"), D22 (profile names and defaults: default \"ship `:workspace`, `:read-only`, `:network`; interactive default stays today's engine defaults until an explicit profile is set; managed policy may force a stricter profile, never a looser one\") and any other decision these units cite; the phase ordering; the peer-project notes at the top, including the Codex re-read on permission profiles and the tool registry). Existing plans for style and depth: `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/AC1_PLAN.md`, `VR2_PLAN.md`, `GV1_PLAN.md`.\n\nThe units, verbatim acceptance from the plan (re-read them there):\n- PF-3: transport beyond a parent's stdio: a unix domain socket (default) with a token and origin check for any localhost HTTP/WebSocket listener; several clients may attach and reattach. Acceptance: a second client attaches to a running session; a wrong token is refused; the socket is mode 0600.\n- PF-4: library entry point: a `lib` entry that does not run commander on import, `exports` and `types` in `cli/package.json`, a package name decision, a documented minimal embedding example that spawns the sidecar. Acceptance: an example script runs a verified task through the entry point in CI.\n- SB-1: named permission profiles that map today's bwrap/docker flags, HOME mounts and network settings without loosening gates; profiles compose with approval mode and managed policy; `nexus doctor` and session posture print the effective profile; document residual holes honestly. Acceptance: CLI and doctor show the effective profile; a profile cannot disable verification-path protection, grant allow-always, or bypass G-3; existing sandbox tests stay green.\n- MT-2: tools are registered spec+handler pairs (a registry: spec and handler bound together); dispatch and G-3 suites green. It follows MT-1 (the `agent.ts` split, landed in v1.2.1): confirm what MT-1's acceptance was and whether anything of MT-1 is left, and say so.\n- PA-2: conflicting units fail verification on the merged tree (\"verified merge arbitration\" for parallel operation units): read the PA-1/PA-3/PA-4 docs under `docs/v1.3.0/` and `cli/src/core/operation*.ts`, `cli/src/batch.ts`, `cli/src/loop.ts`, worktree handling, to define what a \"unit\", a \"merge\" and \"verification on the merged tree\" are here.\n\nExisting building blocks to reuse (verify each by reading; cite file:line): the sidecar server and protocol (`cli/src/sidecar.ts`, `cli/src/protocol.ts`, `docs/protocol/`), `ApplicationCore` (`cli/src/core/app.ts`), the ACP adapter's bounded line reader and request correlator (`cli/src/acp/jsonrpc.ts`), `cli/src/index.ts` (commander program; how it is structured and what importing it does today), `cli/package.json`, `scripts/package-linux.sh` and the package smoke tests, the sandbox (`cli/src/sandbox.ts`, bwrap and docker engines, `cli/test/verify_sandbox.test.ts`, `sandbox_*.test.ts`), `nexus doctor` (`cli/src/doctor.ts`), managed policy (`cli/src/core/managed_policy.ts`, `policy_runtime.ts`, `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md`: `sandbox.*` keys), the capability registry and definitions (`cli/src/core/capability_definitions/`, `capabilityRegistry`, `cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts`, `cli/src/core/spine.ts`, `cli/src/tools.ts`), verification (`cli/src/core/verify*.ts`), operations (`cli/src/core/operation*.ts`).\n\nHard constraints every plan must respect:\n- The approval gates, verification-path protection, managed policy and the VERIFIED outcome ladder are authorities no new surface may weaken (gate G-3: a client, hook, plugin, schedule or policy must not bypass a gate). PF-3 in particular adds a listener: the plan must say who can connect (same uid only, by socket permissions and directory permissions), what the token protects against, what a second client may and may not do (may it answer an approval the first client was asked? decide conservatively), and why DNS-rebinding/CSRF cannot reach a localhost HTTP/WebSocket listener if one exists at all (the conservative decision may be: unix socket only in v1.3.0, no TCP listener; say so if you take it and check it against the acceptance wording).\n- A CI gate fails if `runTurnImpl`, `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl` or `driveOperation` grow in cyclomatic complexity or nesting (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`). MT-2 touches dispatch: plan it so those functions shrink or stay equal, and say how the baseline file is handled if a function is split (read `cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`: a renamed or missing function fails closed).\n- Model-facing tool descriptions and the tool list have a size budget and exact-shape tests (`cli/test/context_convergence.test.ts`, `cli/test/lsp_shape.test.ts`, protocol parity tests): MT-2 must be behaviour-preserving for what the model sees; say which characterization tests pin that and which must be added first.\n- Every new CLI command, setting, protocol message or file format needs docs (gate G-6 wants a runnable example per new surface, run in CI), a schema/version ledger entry where the repo keeps one, tests that fail when the guard is removed, and hostile cases.\n- Tests run with a throwaway HOME, no network, no live inference (scripted gateway fixture `cli/test/helpers/fixtures.ts` `startGateway`); new test files register `cli/test/helpers/leak_guard.ts`; child processes are stopped by pid; sockets and listeners are closed.\n- Linux x86_64 is the only release target. Node 22 and 24.\n- PF-4: importing the library entry must have no side effects (no commander parse, no process.exit, no stdout writes, no reading of the real HOME at import time); the package must still build the same tarball layout (`scripts/package-linux.sh`, reproducibility check: the package is built twice and compared byte for byte in CI); D13 default = not published.\n\nFor EACH unit give: the acceptance verbatim; what exists that it builds on (file:line); the design (data model, commands/flags, protocol or file formats with exact shapes, state on disk and permissions, lifecycle, failure behaviour, limits); the security analysis; the exact list of files to add/change; the tests by name with what each proves (including hostile cases and, for refactors, the characterization tests that must be green before and after); docs and ledgers; and 2–4 mergeable slices in order, each independently green, with rough size (S/M/L) and the files touched, flagging hot files shared with other work (`cli/src/index.ts`, `cli/src/doctor.ts`, `cli/src/sidecar.ts`, `cli/src/core/dispatch_tool_call.ts`, `cli/src/core/run_turn.ts`, `cli/src/config.ts`, `cli/src/types.ts`). Keep each unit to what its acceptance needs and state what is out of scope for v1.3.0. Note dependencies between these units and on BG-1 (detached runs; planned separately: BG-1 may rely on PF-3's socket for live attach) and on GV-1/GV-5 (managed policy; being finished now).\n\nAlso record: a recommended build order across the five units with reasons (smallest and least entangled first; MT-2 is a wide refactor of hot files, so say when it is safest), reference-coding notes per `docs/XERJ_REFERENCE_CODING.md` (brief; \"XERJ unavailable: <reason>\" plus what you used is acceptable), risks, and open questions that could not be settled from the code.\n\nShell notes: zsh (use `bash -c` for loops); output is filtered by `rtk` (prefix `rtk proxy` when a grep/sed pipeline looks odd). Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus.\n\nReturn (under 600 words): the file path; for each unit the slice list with sizes and hot files; the recommended build order; the decisions taken; the main risks; anything you could not determine. Be concrete enough that an implementer can start slice 1 of each unit from the plan alone."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
