{"type":"pr-link","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","prNumber":772,"prUrl":"https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/772","prRepository":"mikeholownych/nexus-code","timestamp":"2026-11-24T09:12:28.211Z"}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","hash":"4edfbcb8e61548b6c62aae311b201d71bba5c78a1436bf8948a768302878bf22","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UEt8PcQu4PWAgfvoYUgaDt","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b/scratchpad/pr_submod.md","content":"Follow-up to #772, which was merged before its review reported. The review that then ran against the release tip (`4ae25a10`) found one way past the check.\n\n## What the probe showed\n\nSeventeen configurations were planted in a throwaway workspace and the real audit export was run on each, once with the check and once with it disabled.\n\n- **Refused with the check, executed without it:** a clean filter, a process filter, a filter from `include.path`, `core.attributesFile`, `.git/info/attributes`, a `.git` file pointing at another git directory, `core.worktree`, and a filter in `config.worktree` with `extensions.worktreeConfig`.\n- **Bypass: a registered submodule.** With a clean filter in `.git/modules/<name>/config` and the attribute in the submodule's `info/attributes`, the planted command ran with the check enabled. The check lists the workspace's own configuration; a submodule is a repository of its own, and `git status` reads its configuration when it looks inside it.\n- **Controls as intended:** a plain repository compares normally; the operator's own global filter is allowed and runs; a repository with a local filter key and no attribute using it is refused (the documented trade-off).\n- **Proved nothing either way** (did not run even without the check): `core.fsmonitor` in two spellings (already disabled on the commands that would run it), a `post-index-change` hook file, an `includeIf` fixture, a subsection crafted to confuse the scope parsing.\n\n## The fix\n\n`repositoryConfiguresPrograms` now also refuses a workspace that has a submodule: an index entry of mode `160000`, or anything stored under the git directory's `modules/`. Both are read without touching the working tree. A listing that fails counts as a hit.\n\nOne defect of the first version is fixed with it: the function returned \"no\" as soon as the workspace's own configuration was clean, so nothing after that line could ever run. I found that when the first attempt at this fix had no effect.\n\nRefusing every workspace with a submodule is blunt. It is the safe answer for a command meant to be run on a workspace one does not trust, and the binding says `unavailable` with the reason. Walking into each submodule and listing its configuration would be finer; it is more code and more git calls in hostile directories, and can follow if anyone needs the comparison for such workspaces.\n\n## Tests\n\nNew test: a workspace with a registered submodule whose own configuration defines a clean filter, the submodule's tracked file re-stamped so git must hash it. The marker file must not exist after the export and the binding must be `unavailable`. With the two submodule lines disabled, the test fails with \"the submodule's configured command ran\".\n\nLocally on Node 24: `audit_export` 21/21, `audit_command` 8/8, `audit_bundle` 25/25, `audit_schema` 7/7, `leak_guard` 6/6, `candidate_git_paths` 36/36, `tsc --noEmit` clean, complexity gate unchanged.\n\n## Still open\n\n- The other callers of the candidate-identity routine (`agent.ts`, `spine_handlers.ts`, `worktree.ts`, `state.ts`, `operation_runner_execution.ts`) run git in the operator's own workspace without this check. That is a different trust situation and needs its own decision.\n- `docs/AUDIT.md` does not yet list `receipts/candidate-binding.json` or the `unavailable` reasons (open from the review of #771).\n- No test for an `includeIf` that actually matches.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EJaGNCp9hetWcfCZBjpP3e","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd ~/nexus-code && u=$(gh pr create --base release/v1.3.0 --head v1/audit-binding-submodules --title \"fix(audit): do not hash a workspace that has a submodule\" --body-file /tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b/scratchpad/pr_submod.md | tail -1); echo \"$u\"; gh pr comment 772 --body \"Post-merge review result (this merged before a review had reported): a probe of seventeen planted configurations against the release tip \\`4ae25a10\\` found the check sound for every configuration that executes without it, **except a registered submodule**, whose own configuration the listing does not see: its clean filter ran with the check enabled. Fix: ${u}. It also removes an early return in this PR's function that would have skipped any later condition.\" | tail -1; echo \"$(date -u +%FT%TZ) PR (claude-invariants-audit): $u from ~/nexus-wt/cgit. The probe of #772 on release tip 4ae25a10 found one bypass: a submodule's own git config (filter in .git/modules/<name>/config) is not listed by the check and its filter ran during audit export. Fix refuses a workspace that has a submodule. Independent review to follow; please do not merge before it is posted.\" >> \"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/nexus-claims.log\"","description":"Open the PR for the submodule fix, note the finding on PR 772, and log it"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
